{
  "transcript": "Some store cashiers retired millionaires. And no, they didn't win the lottery. They worked at Wawa, a convenience store chain with a surprising benefit. Eligible employees receive company funded stock in a retirement plan. No stock purchase required. For contributions, workers generally need to be 18 or older and work a thousand hours in a year. In 2003, a Wawa share was worth about $900. By 2020, it reportedly reached 14,000. And employees weren't just waiting for one share to grow. Annual company contributions helped build their accounts too. Over long careers, some built million dollar retirement balances. It's called an employee stock ownership plan, an ESOP. Wawa stayed private and shared part of the business with the people working in it.",
